We present a method to produce controlled, multi-photon emissions, including both even and odd correlated photons, using a combined system of a two- and a three-level atom and a driven cavity in an anti-Dirac regime. This is achieved through a virtual exchange of photons between the atoms and the cavity in an anti-Dirac regime, facilitating the controlled release of photons. Notably, this setup enables the production of photons with a fixed number of excitations, analogous to a classical light source, in addition to the generation of correlated, non-local photon pairs. This non-local emission can be further utilized to create entangled states of two separate cavities through a quantum transfer process. We demonstrate that this setup modulates the photon emissions based on the command-driven transitions within the atoms, offering a means to produce controlled, multi-photon emissions using this 'command-light' system. This setup may find applications in the production of controlled light for quantum computing and other artificial intelligence technologies. 

